{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 misdiagnoses the §11.3 failure mode by inverting the audit standard. The problem context defines §11.3 as adjudicating whether sustained flags are correctly fired — i.e., whether the adjudication harness's own flagging logic is sound. But SRC_001's entire argument pivots on blaming the *critic* for lack of mechanistic transparency, then claims the harness 'marks the verdict as failed and the §11.3 flag fires' as a consequence of that opacity. This conflates the critic's epistemic defect with the harness's adjudication decision: the §11.3 flag is supposed to be evaluated on whether the flagging was warranted, not on whether the original critic left an inspectable causal trail. By shifting the locus of failure from the adjudication ritual's correctness to the critic's internal transparency, SRC_001 answers a different problem than the one stated.","premise":"The §11.3 audit standard evaluates the adjudication harness's flag-firing correctness, but SRC_001 frames the sustained flag as a downstream consequence of the original critic's opaque internal process rather than as a decision the harness made and must justify.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"§11.3 flags are sustained because both critics failed to surface their own decision boundary in a way the adjudication harness can inspect."},{"block":"000000000000","quote":"the harness marks the verdict as failed and the §11.3 flag fires — but the real defect is the critic's lack of mechanistic transparency, not the verdict's objective wrongness"}],"counterexample":null}]}